package Vixen.Shaders.Vfx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// The shader the CPU particle expansion can actually be drawn with.
//
// `ParticleBillboard` expands the quad *in the vertex stage*: it takes a corner offset and a particle
// — centre, velocity, size, rotation, age — and builds the four corners on the GPU. That is the right
// shape for the compute path, where the particles are already in a buffer the vertex stage can index.
//
// It is the wrong shape for the path the engine has today. `VfxGeometryBuilder` expands on the CPU
// because `Vixen.Vfx` knows nothing about graphics, which is what lets the expansion be tested without
// a device — and what it produces is `ParticleVertex`: a world-space position, a texture coordinate
// and a colour, four of them per particle, already turned to face the camera. No shader in the library
// took those three, so `ParticleRenderFeature` was a renderer with nothing to render with. This is
// that shader.
//
// **A procedural disc rather than an atlas**, which is a decision rather than a shortcut. A round soft
// dot is what an ember, a spark and a dust mote all are, and computing it from the texture coordinate
// costs two multiplies and a `pow` against a texture fetch, an image asset to author, a sampler and
// two more bindings in the set. `ParticleBillboard` is where a flipbook belongs — it already has the
// atlas, the sub-UV blending and the frame interpolation — and a project that wants one has a shader
// for it.
//
// **Set 1 and set 2, and no set 0 at all.** `ParticleRenderFeature.Draw` binds the view block and the
// material's set and nothing else, so a per-frame binding here would be a set the pass never fills and
// a driver refusing every draw. `ShadowCaster` is the same shape for the same reason, and its remarks
// explain why the *name* matters as much as the set: `ViewConstants` writes `viewProjection` at offset
// zero, and set 1 is a contract between shaders rather than any one shader's business.

shader ParticleSprite {
    /// Whether the quad is shaped into a disc, or left as the square the expansion produced.
    ///
    /// On for anything round — embers, sparks, smoke — and off for a particle whose quad *is* the
    /// shape, which is what a square of glass or a screen-space streak wants. A permutation rather
    /// than a `smoothstep` with a parameter that can be set to zero, because off removes the falloff
    /// entirely rather than computing one that does nothing.
    [Permutation] val SoftEdge: bool = true

    /// The camera's, arriving as the *view's* — see the header.
    [PerView] var viewProjection: mat4

    /// Multiplied into every particle's own colour, so one effect can be tinted without respawning it.
    var tint: float4 = float4(1f, 1f, 1f, 1f)

    /// How much light the sprite emits, in the scene's own photometric units.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Not a 0..1 opacity dial.** These are drawn into a linear HDR target that a tonemap and a
    /// bloom chain read, so the number that matters is how bright the thing is against the rest of the
    /// frame — a scene lit in cd/m² needs hundreds or thousands here for a spark to read as hot rather
    /// than as a grey smudge. One is "as bright as the vertex colour said", which is what a
    /// non-photometric project wants.
    var emissive: float = 1f

    /// How fast the disc falls off towards its rim.
    ///
    /// One is a linear cone; higher concentrates the brightness into the middle, which is what makes a
    /// spark look like a point with a glow rather than like a ball. Below one it flattens towards a
    /// hard-edged circle.
    var edgeSharpness: float = 1.6f

    stream var spriteColor: float4
    stream var spriteUv: float2

    /// The three attributes `ParticleVertex` holds, under the names its `VertexSchema` gives them.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The names are the contract. `VertexSchema` matches a mesh's attributes to a stage's inputs by
    /// name rather than by location, which is what lets one buffer feed two passes that number their
    /// locations differently — so `texcoord` here and `uv` in the schema is an attribute the pipeline
    /// refuses to bind, and the driver says only that the layout is incomplete.
    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex(position: float3, texcoord: float2, color: float4): float4 {
        spriteColor = color
        spriteUv = texcoord

        return viewProjection * float4(position, 1f)
    }

    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment(): float4 {
        var alpha = spriteColor.a

        if (SoftEdge) {
            // The squared radius from the quad's centre, so the falloff is round and needs no `sqrt`.
            // Saturated before the `pow` because a corner of the quad is at radius sqrt(2), where the
            // unclamped expression is negative and `pow` of a negative is undefined.
            val offset = spriteUv * 2f - 1f
            val falloff = saturate(1f - dot(offset, offset))

            alpha = alpha * pow(falloff, edgeSharpness)
        }

        // ⚠ Not premultiplied. `BlendState.Additive` is `src × srcAlpha + dst`, so the alpha is applied
        // by the blend — multiplying it in here as well would square it, and a particle at half alpha
        // would come out at a quarter. `ParticleBillboard` returns premultiplied because it is written
        // for `PremultipliedAlpha`; the two are not interchangeable and this is where that is said.
        return float4(spriteColor.rgb * tint.rgb * emissive, alpha * tint.a)
    }
}
